---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/use-cases/back-in-stock';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'use-cases/back-in-stock');
const desc = "Build a waitlist that remembers the requested variant, checks sellable inventory and tells subscribers what an alert actually promises.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Does a back-in-stock email reserve a unit?",
    "answer": "Only if your actual inventory process reserves it. Otherwise explain that availability can change before checkout."
  },
  {
    "question": "When should the alert send?",
    "answer": "After the requested variant is confirmed sellable for the subscriber\u2019s market and the queue allocation permits dispatch. Choose batch intervals from stock and operational capacity."
  },
  {
    "question": "What conversion rate should I target?",
    "answer": "Use your own comparable restocks with explicit audience and observation windows. Stock quantity, wait duration and product demand make a universal target misleading."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16 md:py-24">
    <a class="link" href="/use-cases">← All use cases</a>
    <p class="editorial-kicker mt-6">Shopify operating playbook</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">Back-in-stock alerts: variant accuracy and a fair queue</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">Build a waitlist that remembers the requested variant, checks sellable inventory and tells subscribers what an alert actually promises.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Define the waitlist record</h2>
      <p>Store subscriber identity, product and variant identifiers, market, signup time, capture source and the notification permission recorded at signup. Keep the requested size or shade even if the product name changes. A restock request is not evidence that someone wants every promotional campaign. Explain how to cancel the request and whether an alert reserves inventory.</p>
      <p>Deduplicate repeated requests for the same subscriber, variant and market. Preserve the original queue time unless the customer deliberately cancels and joins again. Multiple capture forms can feed one queue; they must not create independent alert senders.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Separate received inventory from sellable stock</h2>
      <p>Choose the inventory state that operations considers ready to sell. A warehouse receipt, a returned unit awaiting inspection and inventory available to the destination market are different events. Record inventory event time and ingestion time so delayed updates can be investigated.</p>
      <p>Before dispatch, recheck variant availability and shipping eligibility. Use a stock buffer and batch size fitted to allocation, reservations and observed checkout capacity. A positive parent-product count does not prove that the requested variant is available. A send-time check reduces stale alerts; it cannot guarantee availability when the email is opened.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Allocate partial restocks honestly</h2>
      <p>Choose a documented queue policy: signup order, a disclosed early-access tier or another explained allocation. When stock cannot cover the queue, send an initial batch, observe stock and checkout load, then decide whether to release another. Do not promise first access unless execution preserves that order.</p>
      <p>Do not equate a notification with a reservation. If stock is reserved, specify the actual reservation terms and release rule. Otherwise use accurate availability language rather than an invented purchase deadline. Close or pause intake when a fixed production run cannot plausibly serve more requests.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Keep the alert matched to the product</h2>
      <p>Show the requested size, color or shade, a current product image, destination currency and a link selecting the correct variant. Recheck price and product status during message preparation. If the variant disappears, hold the message or use a clearly labeled alternative path rather than silently swapping products.</p>
      <p>For fashion, a different color is an alternative, not fulfillment of a size request. For beauty, distinguish travel size from full size and avoid replacing a shade without explanation. For multi-location stock, validate that the subscriber can actually order from the replenished location.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Handle long waits and model changes</h2>
      <p>If the restock date is unknown, say so. Offer a separate choice to see in-stock alternatives where permission supports that message. Do not describe substitute stock as the requested restock. Buying an alternative need not cancel the original request unless that is the stated policy or the customer chooses it.</p>
      <p>If a product becomes a preorder, explain the new payment and fulfillment terms before transferring anyone into that journey. If it is discontinued, close the waitlist explicitly. A long wait is a reason to confirm interest and information accuracy, not an automatic reason for a discount.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Coordinate cart, browse and launch messages</h2>
      <p>An unavailable item in a cart should not generate a recovery promise that it can be purchased now. Hand off to a permitted waitlist invitation or an accurately described substitute. After an alert leads to a new cart, let the cart workflow own that active purchase intent.</p>
      <p>Suppress duplicate offers for the same variant across senders, but do not suppress unrelated service messages or every cart merely because a customer has a waitlist entry. A launch that shares stock with a restock needs one allocation owner. See the <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">cart contract</a> and <a class="link" href="/use-cases/browse-abandonment">browse handoff</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Measure demand without inventing conversion</h2>
      <p>Report unique eligible waitlisters, unique alerted subscribers, orders containing the requested variant and remaining queue depth. State the observation window and whether the order happened after an alert or without one. Sell-through can reflect low stock as well as strong demand; it is not proof that the email caused purchases.</p>
      <p>Distinguish unavailable-at-dispatch incidents from sold-out-at-click reports. Log inventory snapshots, dispatch times and support reports; support tickets alone do not count all failures. Track duplicate alerts and queue-order exceptions. Use waitlist depth for merchandising with an allowance for stale requests, duplicate identities and bot activity.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Forecast cost and contribution</h2>
      <p>Estimate message volume from eligible queue size and planned batches, including confirmations and any supported reminders. Compare the total capture, integration, sending, support and operator cost with the existing process. Verify billing treatment with each candidate rather than assuming a profile or send plan is always cheaper.</p>
      <p>Calculate order contribution from revenue already net of discounts and refunds, less product and variable fulfillment costs. Subtract messaging and incremental operating costs at program level. Do not subtract the discount twice or assume every waitlist order is incremental. A full-price alert is a useful starting design when the task is availability, not a price promotion.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Rehearse catalog and queue failures</h2>
      <p>Proposed fixtures, not observed merchant results: restock Medium while Large remains unavailable; expect only the matching queue to qualify. Replay the same inventory event; expect no duplicate alert. Exhaust stock between batches; expect the next batch to hold. Deliver an old event after a newer sellout; expect current stock to govern.</p>
      <p>Also test duplicate form submissions, a changed email address, market shipping restrictions, an unsubscribed requester and a purchase arriving before dispatch. Record expected and actual outcomes with timestamps. For a hype drop, review bot intake and use appropriate form abuse controls without silently discarding legitimate queue positions.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Select and hand over the implementation</h2>
      <p>Ask each candidate to demonstrate capture-to-queue identity, variant events, availability rechecks, batch control, cancellation, exports and failure logs on your catalog. Identify which parts are native, configured or custom. A product demo is not evidence of a production-scale result, and a general app rating does not answer these acceptance questions.</p>
      <p>Enable one alert sender after pausing its predecessor and reconciling pending queue entries. Keep a rollback owner, queue export and incident response ready. Review after restocks and catalog changes: queue order, stale data, duplicate sends and stock complaints. Share variant demand with merchandising and reconcile reporting with <a class="link" href="/use-cases/analytics">analytics</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Back-in-stock alerts FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
